Gaudi Contribution Guide
========================

The Gaudi project is open source (see the LICENSE) and welcomes
contributions from experiments and users.

To contribute to Gaudi development you can *fork* the project from
CERN GitLab, where the Gaudi source is held.

Bug fixes and features should be developed in your own branch, then
a *merge request* made back to the upstream Gaudi repository.

By submitting a merge request to the Gaudi repository you agree that
your contribution will be copyrighted to CERN and covered by the Apache 2
licence, as per the LICENSE.


Building the documentation
--------------------------
The documentation can be built locally with `Pixi <https://pixi.sh>`_, in a
small dedicated environment (no need to configure and build Gaudi):

- ``pixi run docs`` builds the Sphinx website (this documentation) and the
  Doxygen documentation (class reference) in ``build/site``, with the same
  layout as the published one (the Doxygen documentation is in the ``doxygen``
  subdirectory, and it is called ``local``);
- ``pixi run docs-serve`` does the same and serves the result on
  http://localhost:8000 (the list of the available versions of the Doxygen
  documentation is not shown if the pages are opened as local files);
- ``pixi run docs-sphinx`` and ``pixi run docs-doxygen`` build only one of the two.

The configuration of Doxygen is in ``docs/doxygen``.


AI coding assistants
--------------------
The use of AI tools is permitted under the following conditions:

- The developer is responsible for reviewing all AI-generated code and
  ensuring compliance with licensing requirements.
- Commits with significant contributions from AI tools should have a
  commit trailer of the form `Assisted-by: [LLM name/version]`.
- The same note (or similar attribution) should also be added to the
  merge request description.
- Comments on merge requests generated by AI tools should be clearly
  marked as such (e.g. as a quote).


Coding Style Guidelines
-----------------------

Code contributions to Gaudi should adhere to the ``clang-format`` rules
(an automatic check is part of the Continuous Integration system).
